parts.problemMd:
Calculate the mass, in kilograms, of 750 dm<sup>3</sup> of sulfur dioxide, measured at room temperature and pressure.

(relative formula mass: SO<sub>2</sub> = 64; 
1 mol of any gas at room temperature and pressure occupies 24 dm<sup>3</sup>)

parts.solutionMd:
**Answer: any two of: general formula; successive members differ by CH₂; similar chemical properties; same functional group.**

A homologous series:

1. can be represented by a **general formula** (e.g. CnH2n for the alkenes);
2. has successive members that **differ by a CH₂ group**;
3. members show **similar chemical properties** (they react in the same way);
4. share the **same functional group**.

Any two of these score the two marks.

**Answer: draw a tall fractionating column with a single entrance near the bottom and several outlets at different heights, fed by crude oil that has been heated and vaporised first.**

Label the sketch like this:

- a furnace or heater drawn underneath the tower with the words 'crude oil is heated and vaporised', feeding the hot vapours into the tower through the one entrance;
- the crude oil entering at the bottom of the tower (its entry point must not be higher than the second-lowest outlet);
- a temperature gradient marked up the column — hottest at the bottom, coolest at the top;
- the kerosene outlet part-way up the tower, clearly above the bottom and below the top, because kerosene condenses in the middle temperature range;
- at least three other fractions labelled in their correct places relative to kerosene: refinery gases leaving at the very top, then petrol and naphtha below them but above kerosene, and diesel oil, fuel oil and bitumen lower down, with bitumen draining from the very bottom.

The idea the sketch must show: each fraction condenses at the height where the column temperature matches its boiling point. Low-boiling fractions rise to the cool top, high-boiling ones drain out near the hot bottom, and kerosene is one of the middle fractions.
